# Man-Wolf

## Lore

The Man-Wolf always starts as a mortal human or D-Bee - someone who sees himself
as a misfit, somebody who does not fit in the world of men. The suffering and
the longing draw ley line energy to him, and it fuses with him and makes him a
creature that is neither human nor animal but both.

He does not fit the world of animals any better. He cannot stomach carrion,
blood or raw meat, and when he looks into a mirror or a pool he sees a sad human
face staring back even in wolf form. Most become loners who live in the
wilderness as huntsmen by day and wolves by night.

The worst give in to the animal and become Devil-Wolves - cruel beastmen ruled
by hatred, who hunt and kill for pleasure and delude themselves with revenge
against everyone who feared or rejected them.

## Alignment

Any, but the majority are unprincipled (25%), anarchist (35%) or miscreant
(25%). A player character is presumed selfish or good.

## GM Notes

**This is the one the book actually recommends.** Of the eight Woodland
Spirits, seven get a permission and this one gets an endorsement: *"This can be
a fun and challenging character to play."* Its heading says Non-Player
Character.

It changes every night whether it wants to or not, and by day it is a quiet,
private, unconfident person. At night, or when angry, it is bold and aggressive.
That swing is the character.

**It has no magic at all** - the only one of the six - and the largest psionic
list, eight powers. And it is the only one the book bothers to give an
experience ladder, which resolves here to the same house default the other five
take anyway.
', 'published', 'data-script'
WHERE NOT EXISTS (SELECT 1 FROM imported_classes WHERE class_id = 'man-wolf');


